{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014cMFFmSjwV96YPnVgMLZQX","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t#!/usr/bin/env python3\n2\t\"\"\"check_contamination.py — detect project-specific references outside allowlist.\n3\t\n4\tSprint 3 Item E.2. Port of check-contamination.sh to Python, using the\n5\tshared _lib.file_walker.FileWalker. Per debate consensus R-VP2, the\n6\tbash script remains as a thin wrapper that invokes this module so\n7\tSprint 4+ can retire the wrapper cleanly.\n8\t\n9\t## What it detects\n10\t\n11\tNFKC-normalized regex match against a pattern built in TWO parts:\n12\t\n13\t    (a) generic placeholder terms, hardcoded below in\n14\t        `_PLACEHOLDER_TERMS` — these ship to adopters as a working\n15\t        template; replace or extend them when forking.\n16\t    (b) PRIVATE terms, read at scan time from the SCANNED repo's\n17\t        `scripts/contamination-terms.txt` (see `_load_private_terms`).\n18\t        That file is deliberately OUTSIDE the install/upgrade manifest,\n19\t        so no maintainer identity is ever written into an adopter's\n20\t        checkout. An adopter may create the same file to guard THEIR\n21\t        own handle / private project names.\n22\t\n23\tWhen the private file is absent the pattern is placeholder-only. That is\n24\tthe EXPECTED adopter state, not a failure.\n25\t\n26\tFiles matching the pattern outside the allowlist fail the check.\n27\t\n28\t## Allowlist\n29\t\n30\tExact paths + glob patterns (see `_ALLOWLIST_*` below). Binary file\n31\tsuffixes are excluded by extension.\n32\t\n33\tOne NEGATIVE exception overrides the allowlist (deny wins over allow):\n34\tper-plan `LEDGER.md` / `LEDGER-ARCHIVE.md` files under `.claude/plans/`\n35\tare ALWAYS scanned, even though `.claude/plans/*` exempts the tree they\n36\tlive in. See `is_never_allowlisted` for why. For that class alone, a file\n37\tthat is not valid UTF-8 is REPORTED rather than skipped — unparseable\n38\tinput is blocked, per the CLAUDE.md §4 security-matcher rule.\n39\t\n40\t## Rule 2 — `personal-path`\n41\t\n42\tA second, independent rule flags an absolute HOME directory\n43\t(`/Users/<name>/...`, `/home/<name>/...`, or the harness slug form\n44\t`-Users-<name>-...`) under `docs/` and `.claude/plans/` — the two\n45\ttrees the term rule above exempts almost wholesale. It reports\n46\t`file:line` and never the matched line itself.\n47\t\n48\tNOTHING is exempt by NAME or by SUFFIX: every in-scope tracked path\n49\tis read and matched the same way, symlinks included (a link is its\n50\ttarget string). Waivers act on a hit that was already found, and\n51\tare explicit: one `<path> | sha256:<64 hex> | <reason>` row in\n52\t`.claude/scripts/contamination-personal-path-allowlist.txt`, which\n53\twaives that file only while its bytes still hash to the recorded\n54\tdigest. There is no second waiver and nothing is earned by a NAME:\n55\ta signed sentinel is waived by such a row like any other file.\n56\tSee the RULE 2 block below for the full contract and its declared\n57\tblind spots.\n58\t\n59\t## Exit codes\n60\t\n61\t- 0 — clean\n62\t- 1 — contamination found (printed to stdout)\n63\t- 2 — fatal error (unreadable INPUT: the terms file, the\n64\t      personal-path allowlist, or a file this scan had to read\n65\t      that exists and cannot be read; or an enumeration of the\n66\t      index the scan cannot prove it covered)\n67\t\"\"\"\n68\t\n69\tfrom __future__ import annotations\n70\t\n71\timport argparse\n72\timport errno\n73\timport hashlib\n74\timport os\n75\timport re\n76\timport stat\n77\timport subprocess\n78\timport sys\n79\timport time\n80\timport unicodedata\n81\tfrom pathlib import Path\n82\tfrom typing import Dict, List, NamedTuple, Optional, Tuple\n83\t\n84\t# Import the shared walker from _lib/\n85\t_HOOKS_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / \"hooks\"\n86\tif str(_HOOKS_DIR) not in sys.path:\n87\t    sys.path.insert(0, str(_HOOKS_DIR))\n88\tfrom _lib.file_walker import FileWalker  # noqa: E402\n89\tfrom _lib._contamination import bind_personal_paths as _bind_personal_paths\n90\tfrom _lib._contamination import personal_path_policy as _personal_path_policy\n91\tfrom _lib._contamination import personal_path_scanner as _personal_path_scanner\n92\tfrom _lib._contamination.term_scan import find_violations as _find_term_violations\n93\t\n94\t\n95\t# Contamination tokens, in TWO parts — see the module docstring.\n96\t#\n97\t#   1. _PLACEHOLDER_TERMS (in code) — generic placeholders, shipped to\n98\t#      adopters as a working template.\n99\t#   2. Private terms loaded from _PRIVATE_TERMS_RELPATH inside the\n100\t#      SCANNED repo. They live outside the delivered tree because this\n101\t#      guard used to be its own contamination vector: it was the only\n102\t#      file in the install/upgrade manifest carrying the maintainer's\n103\t#      real name, and it survived CI by allowlisting\n104\t#      ITSELF. PLAN-183 W2 A7 removed both halves of that defect — the\n105\t#      identity moved out of the delivered file, and the self-exemption\n106\t#      was dropped from _ALLOWLIST_EXACT so this module is now scanned\n107\t#      by its own pattern.\n108\t#      Do NOT relocate the terms file under .claude/scripts/ — that tree\n109\t#      is delivered wholesale by upgrade.sh regardless of extension.\n110\t#\n111\t# Absent file => placeholder-only pattern, silently (the expected adopter\n112\t# state is not an infrastructure failure). A file that EXISTS but cannot\n113\t# be read is an INPUT failure and is surfaced as fatal by main().\n114\t_PLACEHOLDER_TERMS = (\n115\t    r\"acme\\s*[Ll]edger\",\n116\t    r\"example[\\s._\\-]*owner\",\n117\t    r\"Example\\s+Owner\",\n118\t)\n119\t\n120\t_PRIVATE_TERMS_RELPATH = \"scripts/contamination-terms.txt\"\n121\t\n122\t\n123\tdef _load_private_terms(repo_root: Path) -> List[str]:\n124\t    \"\"\"Extra regex alternatives declared by the repo being scanned.\n125\t\n126\t    Returns [] when the file is absent (normal in an adopter checkout).\n127\t    Propagates OSError when the file exists but cannot be read.\n128\t    \"\"\"\n129\t    path = repo_root / _PRIVATE_TERMS_RELPATH\n130\t    # Only real ABSENCE means \"no private terms\". `is_file()` also said False for a\n131\t    # dangling link and a directory, which dropped the whole term list in silence.\n132\t    if not os.path.lexists(path):\n133\t        return []\n134\t    if not stat.S_ISREG(os.stat(path).st_mode):  # a FIFO would block the read below\n135\t        raise OSError(errno.EINVAL, \"not a regular file\")\n136\t    terms: List[str] = []\n137\t    for raw in path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\").splitlines():\n138\t        line = raw.strip()\n139\t        if not line or line.startswith(\"#\"):\n140\t            continue\n141\t        terms.append(line)\n142\t    return terms\n143\t\n144\t\n145\tdef build_pattern(repo_root: Path) -> \"re.Pattern\":\n146\t    \"\"\"Placeholder terms plus any private terms declared by this repo.\n147\t\n148\t    There is deliberately NO module-level constant holding the full\n149\t    pattern: a name that silently omits the private half is the exact\n150\t    false-green shape this indirection exists to remove.\n151\t    \"\"\"\n152\t    alts = list(_PLACEHOLDER_TERMS) + _load_private_terms(repo_root)\n153\t    return re.compile(\"|\".join(alts))\n154\t\n155\t# Allowlist — mirrors the case block in check-contamination.sh\n156\t#\n157\t# Philosophy: the check defends the FRAMEWORK CORE (hooks, scripts,\n158\t# skills/core, skills/frontend, templates) from leaking project-specific\n159\t# references. It does NOT apply to:\n160\t#   - Historical decision records (ADRs carry Accepted-By owner handles)\n161\t#   - Plan artifacts (PLAN-*/ subfolders document adopter context)\n162\t#   - Adopter-facing documentation (docs/ explains framework to adopters)\n163\t#   - Adopter-specific tooling (check-originator-residue, compare-adopters,\n164\t#     adopter-metrics, log-friction — explicitly about adopter workflow)\n165\t#   - Published compliance contract (SPEC/v1/ references concrete examples)\n166\t#   - Benchmarks against named peers (benchmarks/public/vs-*.md)\n167\t#   - Case studies (docs/case-studies/ inherits adopter names by design)\n168\t#   - Issue templates (.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE surfaces project context)\n169\t#   - Historical archives (CLAUDE_FULL.md is the overflow-log for CLAUDE.md)\n170\t_ALLOWLIST_EXACT = {\n171\t    \"LICENSE\",\n172\t    \"CHANGELOG.md\",\n173\t    # ---- S214: audit report + plugin builder reference identity tokens by-design ----\n174\t    \"MORNING-REPORT-S214.md\",   # CTO audit report that AUDITS the Owner-identity leak (must name the tokens)\n175\t    \"REPORT-S225-fable-audit.md\",  # S225 Fable audit: documents identity-leak findings (E5-F10, E7) — same rationale as MORNING-REPORT-S214\n176\t    \"scripts/build-plugin.py\",  # plugin builder: sanitize_paths()/identity_report() match these tokens to strip/report them\n177\t    \"scripts/contamination-terms.txt\",  # the private terms list itself — identity lives here BY DESIGN; never delivered (scripts/ contributes zero manifest files)\n178\t    \".github/workflows/validate.yml\",\n179\t    \".github/CODEOWNERS\",\n180\t    \".claude/scripts/check-contamination.sh\",\n181\t    # NOTE: .claude/scripts/check_contamination.py is deliberately NOT\n182\t    # allowlisted. Self-exemption is what let the maintainer identity sit\n183\t    # inside this very file while CI reported green (PLAN-183 W2 A7). The\n184\t    # module is now scanned by its own pattern; if identity re-enters it,\n185\t    # the guard fails on itself.\n186\t    \".claude/scripts/tests/test_check_contamination.py\",\n187\t    \".claude/hooks/tests/test_check_canonical_edit.py\",\n188\t    \"CLAUDE.md\",\n189\t    \"CLAUDE_FULL.md\",\n190\t    \"RELEASE.md\",\n191\t    \"SECURITY.md\",\n192\t    \"docs/QUICKSTART.md\",\n193\t    \"docs/QUICKSTART.pt-BR.md\",\n194\t    \"docs/GUIA-COMPLETO.md\",\n195\t    \"docs/GUIA-COMPLETO.pt-BR.md\",\n196\t    \"docs/HONEST-LIMITATIONS.md\",\n197\t    \"docs/ROADMAP-CLOSURE.md\",\n198\t    \"docs/threat-model.md\",\n199\t    \"docs/soc2-audit-mapping.md\",\n200\t    \"docs/fixture-budget.md\",\n201\t    \"docs/opus-4-7-baseline.md\",\n202\t    \"docs/opus-4-7-operations.md\",\n203\t    \"docs/opus-4-7-phase6-report.md\",\n204\t    \"docs/UPGRADE-PROCEDURE.md\",\n205\t    \"docs/SLO-SLA.md\",\n206\t    \".claude/scripts/check-framework-updates.sh\",\n207\t    \".claude/scripts/adopter-metrics.py\",\n208\t    \".claude/scripts/compare-adopters.py\",\n209\t    \".claude/scripts/check-originator-residue.py\",\n210\t    \".claude/scripts/log-friction.sh\",\n211\t    \".claude/scripts/tests/test_admin_invite.py\",\n212\t    \".claude/scripts/tests/test_check_originator_residue.py\",\n213\t    \".claude/scripts/tests/test_compare_adopters.py\",\n214\t    \".claude/policies/.drift-manifest.json\",\n215\t    # ---- audit-v2 Wave C-bis hot-fix (2026-04-27) ------------------\n216\t    # Pre-existing legitimate references to the Owner / canonical\n217\t    # repo URL surfaced after CLAUDE.md ADR-count drift was fixed\n218\t    # (which was masking these in CI). Each entry below is a\n219\t    # human-reviewed legitimate reference (Owner attribution in\n220\t    # ceremony scripts, GPG roster, design-intent github URLs, etc).\n221\t    # ----------------------------------------------------------------\n222\t    # Hook-lib files with Owner attribution in docstrings (canonical;\n223\t    # fix would require new sentinel ceremony — defer to future cleanup\n224\t    # ADR; allowlist now to unblock CI):\n225\t    \".claude/hooks/_lib/escalation_signals.py\",\n226\t    \".claude/hooks/_lib/rag_events.py\",\n227\t    \".claude/hooks/check_tier_policy.py\",\n228\t    # Owner GPG fingerprint roster (by design — references Owner's key):\n229\t    \".claude/sentinel-signers.txt\",\n230\t    \".claude/skill-patch-signers.txt\",\n231\t    # Operational docs with design-intent github.com/<owner>/ URLs\n232\t    # (issue tracker, release page, etc.):\n233\t    \"docs/READINESS-STATUS.md\",\n234\t    \"docs/CEO-MODEL-ROUTING.md\",\n235\t    \"docs/ROADMAP.md\",\n236\t    \"docs/SP-NNN-OWNER-WORKFLOW.md\",\n237\t    \"docs/rotation-log.md\",\n238\t    \"docs/SECURITY.md\",\n239\t    # Detector test fixtures reference Owner repo paths in mock-event\n240\t    # bodies (legitimate test data):\n241\t    \".claude/scripts/detectors/tests/fixtures.py\",\n242\t    # SBOM generator hard-codes upstream URL (single source of truth):\n243\t    \".claude/scripts/generate-sbom.py\",\n244\t    # ---- S155 CI-cleanup (2026-05-22) ------------------------------\n245\t    # Same pattern as the 2026-04-27 batch above: pre-existing legitimate\n246\t    # Owner / canonical-repo references that were masked in CI by the\n247\t    # contract/ADR-count/perf red layers, surfaced once those were fixed.\n248\t    # Each is human-reviewed: non-template-content (not shipped to adopters)\n249\t    # OR a canonical-repo reference OR a detector test fixture. NOT personal\n250\t    # contamination in shipped template content.\n251\t    # ----------------------------------------------------------------\n252\t    # LLM03 supply-chain detector treats the framework's CANONICAL repo\n253\t    # (github.com/<owner>/) as trusted alongside github.com/anthropics/ —\n254\t    # adopters clone framework updates from it (canonical-repo reference):\n255\t    \".claude/hooks/_lib/output_scan.py\",\n256\t    # Detector / canonical-edit / mcp-guard test fixtures carry Owner repo\n257\t    # paths in mock-event bodies as legitimate test data (same rationale as\n258\t    # detectors/tests/fixtures.py + replay/tests/* below):\n259\t    \".claude/hooks/tests/test_check_canonical_edit_markers.py\",\n260\t    \".claude/hooks/tests/test_check_canonical_edit_mcp.py\",\n261\t    \".claude/hooks/tests/test_mcp_canonical_guard.py\",\n262\t    \".claude/scripts/tests/test_success_receipt.py\",\n263\t    \"tests/unit/test_output_scan_llm03.py\",\n264\t    # GPG sentinel-signer roster — references Owner key by design (like\n265\t    # sentinel-signers.txt / skill-patch-signers.txt above):\n266\t    \".claude/security/sentinel-signers-registry.yaml\",\n267\t    # Owner brief + internal design docs (Owner attribution / setup paths;\n268\t    # like the operational docs allowlisted above):\n269\t    \"BUNDLE-OWNER-BRIEF.md\",\n270\t    \"docs/GIF-CAPTURE-SPEC.md\",\n271\t    \"docs/PERMISSION-MODEL-DESIGN.md\",\n272\t    \"docs/security-bash-canonical-guards.md\",\n273\t}\n274\t\n275\t_ALLOWLIST_GLOBS = {\n276\t    # NOTE: fnmatch `*` matches across `/` boundaries here (unlike the\n277\t    # `glob` module). A single `*` after the directory prefix is enough\n278\t    # to cover all nested files — no need for `**`.\n279\t    # Plan artifacts (all file types under .claude/plans/, including\n280\t    # WAR-ROOM/, SPRINT-NN-ROADMAP.md, PLAN-NNN-*.md, PLAN-NNN/...).\n281\t    # Wave C-bis (2026-04-27): broadened from `PLAN-*.md` + `PLAN-*`\n282\t    # to `*` so non-PLAN- artifacts (WAR-ROOM, SPRINT-NN, README) get\n283\t    # covered without per-file additions.\n284\t    \".claude/plans/*\",\n285\t    # Domain squads (by design — each domain lists real-world owners)\n286\t    \".claude/skills/domains/*\",\n287\t    # CI workflow templates distributed to adopters\n288\t    \"templates/.github/workflows/*\",\n289\t    # NPM shim — URLs reference canonical repo owner\n290\t    \"npm/*\",\n291\t    # ADRs — architectural decision records carry Owner Accepted-By\n292\t    \".claude/adr/ADR-*.md\",\n293\t    # Published SPEC — concrete examples reference owner/projects\n294\t    \"SPEC/*\",\n295\t    # Docs subfolders that document the framework's ecosystem:\n296\t    # case studies, research (external competitive analysis),\n297\t    # site HTML, etc.\n298\t    \"docs/case-studies/*\",\n299\t    \"docs/research/*\",\n300\t    \"docs/site/*\",\n301\t    # Benchmarks against named peers\n302\t    \"benchmarks/*\",\n303\t    # Issue templates\n304\t    \".github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/*\",\n305\t    # Owner ceremony archive (Wave C moved 17 OWNER-*.sh here; each\n306\t    # script references Owner's GPG key + project paths by design):\n307\t    \".claude/scripts/owner-ceremony/*\",\n308\t    # Forensic ceremony archive — historical Owner ceremony scripts\n309\t    # retained for chain-of-custody. Never re-executed. PLAN-063 S5.\n310\t    \"scripts/local/historical/*\",\n311\t    # Architect ceremony sentinels — GPG-signed `approved.md` per round\n312\t    # MUST carry Owner handle + key fingerprint by design (the sentinel IS\n313\t    # the canonical Owner authorization artifact). PLAN-072 + PLAN-069 Wave D\n314\t    # surfaced this as latent debt (S81 2026-05-03).\n315\t    \".claude/architect/*\",\n316\t    # Replay redaction test corpus — fixtures + tests in\n317\t    # .claude/scripts/replay/tests/ MUST contain `/Users/<user>/` and\n318\t    # similar OS-path shapes because they are the regression markers proving\n319\t    # the redactor strips them. Removing the literals defeats the test.\n320\t    # PLAN-069 Phase 1 Wave A+B canonical test surface (S81 2026-05-03).\n321\t    \".claude/scripts/replay/tests/*\",\n322\t    # ---- S155 CI-cleanup (2026-05-22) — non-template-content zones ----\n323\t    # Owner-run ceremony / restart tooling at repo root + scripts/local/ +\n324\t    # the .claude mirror. Reference Owner path/key by design; never shipped\n325\t    # to adopters (extends the existing scripts/local/historical/* +\n326\t    # owner-ceremony/* allowlist):\n327\t    \"scripts/local/*\",\n328\t    \".claude/scripts/local/*\",\n329\t    \"OWNER-*.sh\",\n330\t    # Historical Owner-ceremony archive at repo root (archive/): retired\n331\t    # ceremony scripts (OWNER-*-CEREMONY.sh) + the Owner bundle brief moved\n332\t    # here for chain-of-custody. Each references the Owner path / @handle /\n333\t    # GPG key by design; never re-executed, never shipped to adopters\n334\t    # (extends owner-ceremony/* + scripts/local/historical/*). S165 CI-green.\n335\t    \"archive/*\",\n336\t    # Detector test corpora — ATLAS + red-team fixtures MUST carry realistic\n337\t    # /Users/<owner>/ + canonical-repo-URL shapes because they are the\n338\t    # regression markers proving the LLM03 supply-chain + secret/path\n339\t    # detectors fire (same rationale as .claude/scripts/replay/tests/*):\n340\t    \"tests/fixtures/atlas/*\",\n341\t    \"tests/fixtures/red-team-corpus/*\",\n342\t}\n343\t\n344\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n345\t# NEGATIVE exception to the allowlist — deny WINS over allow\n346\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n347\t# `.claude/plans/*` in _ALLOWLIST_GLOBS exempts the plan tree WHOLESALE\n348\t# (fnmatch's `*` crosses `/`, as the note on that set says). That exemption\n349\t# was written for plan PROSE: hand-authored artifacts a maintainer reads\n350\t# before committing, which legitimately name adopter context.\n351\t#\n352\t# PLAN-179 W2 puts a different KIND of file in the same tree. A per-plan\n353\t# `LEDGER.md` is written INCREMENTALLY, mid-session, by the model, from\n354\t# material that includes agent returns and tool output — and this repository\n355\t# is PUBLIC. A blanket exemption over a machine-appended file is a standing\n356\t# leak path, not a reviewed one, and \"the model was told to write\n357\t# identifiers only\" is an instruction, not a control.\n358\t#\n359\t# The cure is a NEGATIVE exception for that ONE class rather than \"add\n360\t# coverage\": dropping `.claude/plans/*` would drag every plan artifact into\n361\t# the scan and regrow exactly the noise the exemption exists to remove.\n362\t#\n363\t# Matched by BASENAME under `.claude/plans/`, at ANY depth — deliberately\n364\t# NOT by glob. `.claude/plans/*/LEDGER.md` misses both\n365\t# `.claude/plans/LEDGER.md` and a ledger parked one directory deeper, and a\n366\t# rule an author sidesteps by moving the file is not a rule.\n367\t_NEVER_ALLOWLISTED_BASENAMES = frozenset({\n368\t    \"LEDGER.md\",          # check_ledger_checkpoint.LEDGER_BASENAME\n369\t    \"LEDGER-ARCHIVE.md\",  # check_ledger_checkpoint.LEDGER_ARCHIVE_BASENAME\n370\t})\n371\t_NEVER_ALLOWLISTED_ROOT = (\".claude\", \"plans\")\n372\t\n373\t\n374\tdef is_never_allowlisted(rel_path: str) -> bool:\n375\t    \"\"\"True for a file the allowlist must NOT be able to exempt.\n376\t\n377\t    ``rel_path`` is repo-relative and POSIX-separated. Kept a pure\n378\t    function so the test can drive it directly and `scan()` can consult\n379\t    it BEFORE the walker's allowlist — deny wins over allow.\n380\t    \"\"\"\n381\t    parts = rel_path.split(\"/\")\n382\t    root = _NEVER_ALLOWLISTED_ROOT\n383\t    return (\n384\t        len(parts) > len(root)\n385\t        and tuple(parts[: len(root)]) == root\n386\t        and parts[-1] in _NEVER_ALLOWLISTED_BASENAMES\n387\t    )\n388\t\n389\t\n390\t# Suffixes to skip (binary / non-text files)\n391\t_SKIP_SUFFIXES = {\n392\t    \".png\", \".jpg\", \".jpeg\", \".gif\", \".webp\",\n393\t    \".pdf\", \".ico\", \".zip\",\n394\t}\n395\t\n396\t\n397\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n398\t\n399\t\n400\t# Rule 2 policy data lives with its detailed rationale; keep every mutable\n401\t# symbol exposed on this facade for established monkeypatch-based callers.\n402\tfrom _lib._contamination import personal_constants as _personal_constants\n403\tfor _key, _value in vars(_personal_constants).items():\n404\t    if _key.startswith(\"_PERSONAL_PATH_\"):\n405\t        globals()[_key] = _value\n406\tdel _key, _value\n407\t\n408\tclass PersonalPathUsage(Exception):\n409\t    \"\"\"A CLI token this gate refuses to guess at — fatal, never ignored.\"\"\"\n410\t\n411\t\n412\tclass _PersonalPathParser(argparse.ArgumentParser):\n413\t    \"\"\"An `argparse` that refuses through THIS module's boundary.\n414\t\n415\t    `ArgumentParser.error()` writes its message to stderr and exits 2,\n416\t    and that message QUOTES the offending token verbatim — measured:\n417\t    `--emit-personal-path-rows=<value>` prints\n418\t    `ignored explicit argument '<value>'`, and a value on this CLI can\n419\t    BE an absolute home path (a mistyped `--root`, a stray positional).\n420\t    A gate that refuses to quote the leak it FOUND must not quote one\n421\t    while refusing to RUN; this was the fourth instance of that shape,\n422\t    and it is cured where the other three were: at the boundary, by the\n423\t    rule's own predicate.\n424\t\n425\t    Precision is traded for containment on purpose. `_personal_path_safe`\n426\t    replaces the WHOLE message when it names a home directory, so the\n427\t    operator loses the token and keeps the rc — which is the same bargain\n428\t    every other print site in this rule makes.\n429\t    \"\"\"\n430\t\n431\t    def error(self, message):        # argparse hook; always raises\n432\t        raise PersonalPathUsage(_personal_path_safe(message))\n433\t\n434\t\n435\t#: `git ls-files -s` mode of a submodule entry: it has no blob in this repository,\n436\t#: so it is the one mode RULE 1 releases by name (and only this one).\n437\t_TERM_GITLINK_MODE = \"160000\"\n438\t\n439\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n440\t# RULE 1 (`scan`) — census of the borders where it could answer \"clean\" without\n441\t# having looked, taken before changing it (PLAN-186-FOLLOWUP AC-7; item 11 of the\n442\t# 1.4.4 backlog: the pack fell twice in the rail, by edges AC-7 had not listed).\n443\t# CURED, each with a test named test_fu144_* in test_check_contamination_personal_path.py:\n444\t#   * empty or partial walk: FileWalker turns a git failure into an empty iterator.\n445\t#     The set of paths seen must COVER the index (a set, not a count, for the reason\n446\t#     RULE 2 paid for in pair-rail S344 round 3);\n447\t#   * an index that cannot be read (git missing, timeout, rc != 0): refused;\n448\t#   * a name that is not valid UTF-8: both sides of the proof decode with\n449\t#     errors=\"replace\", so two such names collapse into one and the sets compare equal\n450\t#     over an unread file. Refused over the WHOLE index before any comparison; RULE 2's\n451\t#     scope exception does not apply, because this rule's scope is every tracked path;\n452\t#   * a tracked symlink (120000): its blob is the target STRING. read_bytes() followed\n453\t#     it, so a term in the payload was never read and someone else's file was judged;\n454\t#   * an index mode other than file, executable, link or gitlink: refused;\n455\t#   * a file that exists and cannot be read, or a non-regular inode at a tracked path\n456\t#     (a FIFO used to hang the read): rc 2. This reverses a deliberate \"fail-OPEN,\n457\t#     INFRASTRUCTURE\" note; it is a gate, not a hook, as _personal_path_assert_enumerable\n458\t#     already argues, so nothing here blocks a session;\n459\t#   * a terms file that is not a regular file was read as absent (`_load_private_terms`).\n460\t# DECLARED, not cured:\n461\t#   * one stray non-UTF-8 byte still makes this rule skip an ordinary file whole (a term\n462\t#     beside it goes unseen). Outside the LEDGER class that is a pinned decision, not an\n463\t#     oversight: test_an_undecodable_non_plan_file_is_still_skipped calls it what keeps\n464\t#     the exception narrow. Changing it is the Owner's call, not this pack's;\n465\t#   * the path allowlist remains an exemption; all other indexed blobs are scanned;\n466\t#   * a UTF-16 document does not match;\n467\t#   * the index is enumerated and the worktree is read, as in RULE 2's blind spots;\n468\t#   * a U+FFFD that is really in a tracked name is refused too (the safe direction).\n469\t# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n470\t\n471\t\n472\t# Export extracted declarations, binding their code to the facade globals.\n473\t# Fail-closed index proofs and monkeypatch interception retain one namespace.\n474\tfrom types import FunctionType as _FunctionType\n475\tfrom _lib._contamination import (\n476\t    personal_facade_io as _personal_facade_io,\n477\t    personal_reporting as _personal_reporting,\n478\t    cli_checks as _cli_checks,\n479\t)\n480\tfor _component in (_personal_facade_io, _personal_reporting, _cli_checks):\n481\t    for _name, _original in vars(_component).items():\n482\t        if isinstance(_original, _FunctionType) and _original.__module__ == _component.__name__:\n483\t            _bound = _FunctionType(\n484\t                _original.__code__, globals(), _original.__name__,\n485\t                _original.__defaults__, _original.__closure__,\n486\t            )\n487\t            _bound.__kwdefaults__ = _original.__kwdefaults__\n488\t            _bound.__annotations__ = dict(_original.__annotations__)\n489\t            _bound.__dict__.update(_original.__dict__)\n490\t            _bound.__qualname__ = _original.__qualname__\n491\t            _bound.__module__ = __name__\n492\t            globals()[_name] = _bound\n493\t        elif isinstance(_original, type) and _original.__module__ == _component.__name__:\n494\t            globals()[_name] = _original\n495\t\n496\t_bind_personal_paths(globals(), _personal_path_scanner)\n497\t_bind_personal_paths(globals(), _personal_path_policy)\n498\t_personal_path_verdict_impl = personal_path_verdict\n499\t# Publish the wrapper over the original policy implementation.\n500\t_original = _personal_facade_io.personal_path_verdict\n501\t_bound = _FunctionType(_original.__code__, globals(), _original.__name__,\n502\t                       _original.__defaults__, _original.__closure__)\n503\t_bound.__annotations__ = dict(_original.__annotations__)\n504\t_bound.__module__ = __name__\n505\tpersonal_path_verdict = _bound\n506\tdel _component, _name, _original, _bound\n507\t\n508\tif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n509\t    sys.exit(main())\n510\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"726e47cb-bee7-4c9e-a147-89aac712ff1d","uuid":"c16af7d7-2d91-4e6f-b13e-c3feea9ae095","timestamp":"2026-11-15T09:33:54.352Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/9n/hm9srl0s1nz45_p12tj61pd80000gn/T/kit-repass-tree.tG8bbW/tree/.claude/scripts/check_contamination.py","content":"#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"check_contamination.py — detect project-specific references outside allowlist.\n\nSprint 3 Item E.2. Port of check-contamination.sh to Python, using the\nshared _lib.file_walker.FileWalker. Per debate consensus R-VP2, the\nbash script remains as a thin wrapper that invokes this module so\nSprint 4+ can retire the wrapper cleanly.\n\n## What it detects\n\nNFKC-normalized regex match against a pattern built in TWO parts:\n\n    (a) generic placeholder terms, hardcoded below in\n        `_PLACEHOLDER_TERMS` — these ship to adopters as a working\n        template; replace or extend them when forking.\n    (b) PRIVATE terms, read at scan time from the SCANNED repo's\n        `scripts/contamination-terms.txt` (see `_load_private_terms`).\n        That file is deliberately OUTSIDE the install/upgrade manifest,\n        so no maintainer identity is ever written into an adopter's\n        checkout. An adopter may create the same file to guard THEIR\n        own handle / private project names.\n\nWhen the private file is absent the pattern is placeholder-only. That is\nthe EXPECTED adopter state, not a failure.\n\nFiles matching the pattern outside the allowlist fail the check.\n\n## Allowlist\n\nExact paths + glob patterns (see `_ALLOWLIST_*` below). Binary file\nsuffixes are excluded by extension.\n\nOne NEGATIVE exception overrides the allowlist (deny wins over allow):\nper-plan `LEDGER.md` / `LEDGER-ARCHIVE.md` files under `.claude/plans/`\nare ALWAYS scanned, even though `.claude/plans/*` exempts the tree they\nlive in. See `is_never_allowlisted` for why. For that class alone, a file\nthat is not valid UTF-8 is REPORTED rather than skipped — unparseable\ninput is blocked, per the CLAUDE.md §4 security-matcher rule.\n\n## Rule 2 — `personal-path`\n\nA second, independent rule flags an absolute HOME directory\n(`/Users/<name>/...`, `/home/<name>/...`, or the harness slug form\n`-Users-<name>-...`) under `docs/` and `.claude/plans/` — the two\ntrees the term rule above exempts almost wholesale. It reports\n`file:line` and never the matched line itself.\n\nNOTHING is exempt by NAME or by SUFFIX: every in-scope tracked path\nis read and matched the same way, symlinks included (a link is its\ntarget string). Waivers act on a hit that was already found, and\nare explicit: one `<path> | sha256:<64 hex> | <reason>` row in\n`.claude/scripts/contamination-personal-path-allowlist.txt`, which\nwaives that file only while its bytes still hash to the recorded\ndigest. There is no second waiver and nothing is earned by a NAME:\na signed sentinel is waived by such a row like any other file.\nSee the RULE 2 block below for the full contract and its declared\nblind spots.\n\n## Exit codes\n\n- 0 — clean\n- 1 — contamination found (printed to stdout)\n- 2 — fatal error (unreadable INPUT: the terms file, the\n      personal-path allowlist, or a file this scan had to read\n      that exists and cannot be read; or an enumeration of the\n      index the scan cannot prove it covered)\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport argparse\nimport errno\nimport hashlib\nimport os\nimport re\nimport stat\nimport subprocess\nimport sys\nimport time\nimport unicodedata\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom typing import Dict, List, NamedTuple, Optional, Tuple\n\n# Import the shared walker from _lib/\n_HOOKS_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent / \"hooks\"\nif str(_HOOKS_DIR) not in sys.path:\n    sys.path.insert(0, str(_HOOKS_DIR))\nfrom _lib.file_walker import FileWalker  # noqa: E402\nfrom _lib._contamination import bind_personal_paths as _bind_personal_paths\nfrom _lib._contamination import personal_path_policy as _personal_path_policy\nfrom _lib._contamination import personal_path_scanner as _personal_path_scanner\nfrom _lib._contamination.term_scan import find_violations as _find_term_violations\n\n\n# Contamination tokens, in TWO parts — see the module docstring.\n#\n#   1. _PLACEHOLDER_TERMS (in code) — generic placeholders, shipped to\n#      adopters as a working template.\n#   2. Private terms loaded from _PRIVATE_TERMS_RELPATH inside the\n#      SCANNED repo. They live outside the delivered tree because this\n#      guard used to be its own contamination vector: it was the only\n#      file in the install/upgrade manifest carrying the maintainer's\n#      real name, and it survived CI by allowlisting\n#      ITSELF. PLAN-183 W2 A7 removed both halves of that defect — the\n#      identity moved out of the delivered file, and the self-exemption\n#      was dropped from _ALLOWLIST_EXACT so this module is now scanned\n#      by its own pattern.\n#      Do NOT relocate the terms file under .claude/scripts/ — that tree\n#      is delivered wholesale by upgrade.sh regardless of extension.\n#\n# Absent file => placeholder-only pattern, silently (the expected adopter\n# state is not an infrastructure failure). A file that EXISTS but cannot\n# be read is an INPUT failure and is surfaced as fatal by main().\n_PLACEHOLDER_TERMS = (\n    r\"acme\\s*[Ll]edger\",\n    r\"example[\\s._\\-]*owner\",\n    r\"Example\\s+Owner\",\n)\n\n_PRIVATE_TERMS_RELPATH = \"scripts/contamination-terms.txt\"\n\n\ndef _load_private_terms(repo_root: Path) -> List[str]:\n    \"\"\"Extra regex alternatives declared by the repo being scanned.\n\n    Returns [] when the file is absent (normal in an adopter checkout).\n    Propagates OSError when the file exists but cannot be read.\n    \"\"\"\n    path = repo_root / _PRIVATE_TERMS_RELPATH\n    # Only real ABSENCE means \"no private terms\". `is_file()` also said False for a\n    # dangling link and a directory, which dropped the whole term list in silence.\n    if not os.path.lexists(path):\n        return []\n    if not stat.S_ISREG(os.stat(path).st_mode):  # a FIFO would block the read below\n        raise OSError(errno.EINVAL, \"not a regular file\")\n    terms: List[str] = []\n    for raw in path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\").splitlines():\n        line = raw.strip()\n        if not line or line.startswith(\"#\"):\n            continue\n        terms.append(line)\n    return terms\n\n\ndef build_pattern(repo_root: Path) -> \"re.Pattern\":\n    \"\"\"Placeholder terms plus any private terms declared by this repo.\n\n    There is deliberately NO module-level constant holding the full\n    pattern: a name that silently omits the private half is the exact\n    false-green shape this indirection exists to remove.\n    \"\"\"\n    alts = list(_PLACEHOLDER_TERMS) + _load_private_terms(repo_root)\n    return re.compile(\"|\".join(alts))\n\n# Allowlist — mirrors the case block in check-contamination.sh\n#\n# Philosophy: the check defends the FRAMEWORK CORE (hooks, scripts,\n# skills/core, skills/frontend, templates) from leaking project-specific\n# references. It does NOT apply to:\n#   - Historical decision records (ADRs carry Accepted-By owner handles)\n#   - Plan artifacts (PLAN-*/ subfolders document adopter context)\n#   - Adopter-facing documentation (docs/ explains framework to adopters)\n#   - Adopter-specific tooling (check-originator-residue, compare-adopters,\n#     adopter-metrics, log-friction — explicitly about adopter workflow)\n#   - Published compliance contract (SPEC/v1/ references concrete examples)\n#   - Benchmarks against named peers (benchmarks/public/vs-*.md)\n#   - Case studies (docs/case-studies/ inherits adopter names by design)\n#   - Issue templates (.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE surfaces project context)\n#   - Historical archives (CLAUDE_FULL.md is the overflow-log for CLAUDE.md)\n_ALLOWLIST_EXACT = {\n    \"LICENSE\",\n    \"CHANGELOG.md\",\n    # ---- S214: audit report + plugin builder reference identity tokens by-design ----\n    \"MORNING-REPORT-S214.md\",   # CTO audit report that AUDITS the Owner-identity leak (must name the tokens)\n    \"REPORT-S225-fable-audit.md\",  # S225 Fable audit: documents identity-leak findings (E5-F10, E7) — same rationale as MORNING-REPORT-S214\n    \"scripts/build-plugin.py\",  # plugin builder: sanitize_paths()/identity_report() match these tokens to strip/report them\n    \"scripts/contamination-terms.txt\",  # the private terms list itself — identity lives here BY DESIGN; never delivered (scripts/ contributes zero manifest files)\n    \".github/workflows/validate.yml\",\n    \".github/CODEOWNERS\",\n    \".claude/scripts/check-contamination.sh\",\n    # NOTE: .claude/scripts/check_contamination.py is deliberately NOT\n    # allowlisted. Self-exemption is what let the maintainer identity sit\n    # inside this very file while CI reported green (PLAN-183 W2 A7). The\n    # module is now scanned by its own pattern; if identity re-enters it,\n    # the guard fails on itself.\n    \".claude/scripts/tests/test_check_contamination.py\",\n    \".claude/hooks/tests/test_check_canonical_edit.py\",\n    \"CLAUDE.md\",\n    \"CLAUDE_FULL.md\",\n    \"RELEASE.md\",\n    \"SECURITY.md\",\n    \"docs/QUICKSTART.md\",\n    \"docs/QUICKSTART.pt-BR.md\",\n    \"docs/GUIA-COMPLETO.md\",\n    \"docs/GUIA-COMPLETO.pt-BR.md\",\n    \"docs/HONEST-LIMITATIONS.md\",\n    \"docs/ROADMAP-CLOSURE.md\",\n    \"docs/threat-model.md\",\n    \"docs/soc2-audit-mapping.md\",\n    \"docs/fixture-budget.md\",\n    \"docs/opus-4-7-baseline.md\",\n    \"docs/opus-4-7-operations.md\",\n    \"docs/opus-4-7-phase6-report.md\",\n    \"docs/UPGRADE-PROCEDURE.md\",\n    \"docs/SLO-SLA.md\",\n    \".claude/scripts/check-framework-updates.sh\",\n    \".claude/scripts/adopter-metrics.py\",\n    \".claude/scripts/compare-adopters.py\",\n    \".claude/scripts/check-originator-residue.py\",\n    \".claude/scripts/log-friction.sh\",\n    \".claude/scripts/tests/test_admin_invite.py\",\n    \".claude/scripts/tests/test_check_originator_residue.py\",\n    \".claude/scripts/tests/test_compare_adopters.py\",\n    \".claude/policies/.drift-manifest.json\",\n    # ---- audit-v2 Wave C-bis hot-fix (2026-04-27) ------------------\n    # Pre-existing legitimate references to the Owner / canonical\n    # repo URL surfaced after CLAUDE.md ADR-count drift was fixed\n    # (which was masking these in CI). Each entry below is a\n    # human-reviewed legitimate reference (Owner attribution in\n    # ceremony scripts, GPG roster, design-intent github URLs, etc).\n    # ----------------------------------------------------------------\n    # Hook-lib files with Owner attribution in docstrings (canonical;\n    # fix would require new sentinel ceremony — defer to future cleanup\n    # ADR; allowlist now to unblock CI):\n    \".claude/hooks/_lib/escalation_signals.py\",\n    \".claude/hooks/_lib/rag_events.py\",\n    \".claude/hooks/check_tier_policy.py\",\n    # Owner GPG fingerprint roster (by design — references Owner's key):\n    \".claude/sentinel-signers.txt\",\n    \".claude/skill-patch-signers.txt\",\n    # Operational docs with design-intent github.com/<owner>/ URLs\n    # (issue tracker, release page, etc.):\n    \"docs/READINESS-STATUS.md\",\n    \"docs/CEO-MODEL-ROUTING.md\",\n    \"docs/ROADMAP.md\",\n    \"docs/SP-NNN-OWNER-WORKFLOW.md\",\n    \"docs/rotation-log.md\",\n    \"docs/SECURITY.md\",\n    # Detector test fixtures reference Owner repo paths in mock-event\n    # bodies (legitimate test data):\n    \".claude/scripts/detectors/tests/fixtures.py\",\n    # SBOM generator hard-codes upstream URL (single source of truth):\n    \".claude/scripts/generate-sbom.py\",\n    # ---- S155 CI-cleanup (2026-05-22) ------------------------------\n    # Same pattern as the 2026-04-27 batch above: pre-existing legitimate\n    # Owner / canonical-repo references that were masked in CI by the\n    # contract/ADR-count/perf red layers, surfaced once those were fixed.\n    # Each is human-reviewed: non-template-content (not shipped to adopters)\n    # OR a canonical-repo reference OR a detector test fixture. NOT personal\n    # contamination in shipped template content.\n    # ----------------------------------------------------------------\n    # LLM03 supply-chain detector treats the framework's CANONICAL repo\n    # (github.com/<owner>/) as trusted alongside github.com/anthropics/ —\n    # adopters clone framework updates from it (canonical-repo reference):\n    \".claude/hooks/_lib/output_scan.py\",\n    # Detector / canonical-edit / mcp-guard test fixtures carry Owner repo\n    # paths in mock-event bodies as legitimate test data (same rationale as\n    # detectors/tests/fixtures.py + replay/tests/* below):\n    \".claude/hooks/tests/test_check_canonical_edit_markers.py\",\n    \".claude/hooks/tests/test_check_canonical_edit_mcp.py\",\n    \".claude/hooks/tests/test_mcp_canonical_guard.py\",\n    \".claude/scripts/tests/test_success_receipt.py\",\n    \"tests/unit/test_output_scan_llm03.py\",\n    # GPG sentinel-signer roster — references Owner key by design (like\n    # sentinel-signers.txt / skill-patch-signers.txt above):\n    \".claude/security/sentinel-signers-registry.yaml\",\n    # Owner brief + internal design docs (Owner attribution / setup paths;\n    # like the operational docs allowlisted above):\n    \"BUNDLE-OWNER-BRIEF.md\",\n    \"docs/GIF-CAPTURE-SPEC.md\",\n    \"docs/PERMISSION-MODEL-DESIGN.md\",\n    \"docs/security-bash-canonical-guards.md\",\n}\n\n_ALLOWLIST_GLOBS = {\n    # NOTE: fnmatch `*` matches across `/` boundaries here (unlike the\n    # `glob` module). A single `*` after the directory prefix is enough\n    # to cover all nested files — no need for `**`.\n    # Plan artifacts (all file types under .claude/plans/, including\n    # WAR-ROOM/, SPRINT-NN-ROADMAP.md, PLAN-NNN-*.md, PLAN-NNN/...).\n    # Wave C-bis (2026-04-27): broadened from `PLAN-*.md` + `PLAN-*`\n    # to `*` so non-PLAN- artifacts (WAR-ROOM, SPRINT-NN, README) get\n    # covered without per-file additions.\n    \".claude/plans/*\",\n    # Domain squads (by design — each domain lists real-world owners)\n    \".claude/skills/domains/*\",\n    # CI workflow templates distributed to adopters\n    \"templates/.github/workflows/*\",\n    # NPM shim — URLs reference canonical repo owner\n    \"npm/*\",\n    # ADRs — architectural decision records carry Owner Accepted-By\n    \".claude/adr/ADR-*.md\",\n    # Published SPEC — concrete examples reference owner/projects\n    \"SPEC/*\",\n    # Docs subfolders that document the framework's ecosystem:\n    # case studies, research (external competitive analysis),\n    # site HTML, etc.\n    \"docs/case-studies/*\",\n    \"docs/research/*\",\n    \"docs/site/*\",\n    # Benchmarks against named peers\n    \"benchmarks/*\",\n    # Issue templates\n    \".github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/*\",\n    # Owner ceremony archive (Wave C moved 17 OWNER-*.sh here; each\n    # script references Owner's GPG key + project paths by design):\n    \".claude/scripts/owner-ceremony/*\",\n    # Forensic ceremony archive — historical Owner ceremony scripts\n    # retained for chain-of-custody. Never re-executed. PLAN-063 S5.\n    \"scripts/local/historical/*\",\n    # Architect ceremony sentinels — GPG-signed `approved.md` per round\n    # MUST carry Owner handle + key fingerprint by design (the sentinel IS\n    # the canonical Owner authorization artifact). PLAN-072 + PLAN-069 Wave D\n    # surfaced this as latent debt (S81 2026-05-03).\n    \".claude/architect/*\",\n    # Replay redaction test corpus — fixtures + tests in\n    # .claude/scripts/replay/tests/ MUST contain `/Users/<user>/` and\n    # similar OS-path shapes because they are the regression markers proving\n    # the redactor strips them. Removing the literals defeats the test.\n    # PLAN-069 Phase 1 Wave A+B canonical test surface (S81 2026-05-03).\n    \".claude/scripts/replay/tests/*\",\n    # ---- S155 CI-cleanup (2026-05-22) — non-template-content zones ----\n    # Owner-run ceremony / restart tooling at repo root + scripts/local/ +\n    # the .claude mirror. Reference Owner path/key by design; never shipped\n    # to adopters (extends the existing scripts/local/historical/* +\n    # owner-ceremony/* allowlist):\n    \"scripts/local/*\",\n    \".claude/scripts/local/*\",\n    \"OWNER-*.sh\",\n    # Historical Owner-ceremony archive at repo root (archive/): retired\n    # ceremony scripts (OWNER-*-CEREMONY.sh) + the Owner bundle brief moved\n    # here for chain-of-custody. Each references the Owner path / @handle /\n    # GPG key by design; never re-executed, never shipped to adopters\n    # (extends owner-ceremony/* + scripts/local/historical/*). S165 CI-green.\n    \"archive/*\",\n    # Detector test corpora — ATLAS + red-team fixtures MUST carry realistic\n    # /Users/<owner>/ + canonical-repo-URL shapes because they are the\n    # regression markers proving the LLM03 supply-chain + secret/path\n    # detectors fire (same rationale as .claude/scripts/replay/tests/*):\n    \"tests/fixtures/atlas/*\",\n    \"tests/fixtures/red-team-corpus/*\",\n}\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# NEGATIVE exception to the allowlist — deny WINS over allow\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# `.claude/plans/*` in _ALLOWLIST_GLOBS exempts the plan tree WHOLESALE\n# (fnmatch's `*` crosses `/`, as the note on that set says). That exemption\n# was written for plan PROSE: hand-authored artifacts a maintainer reads\n# before committing, which legitimately name adopter context.\n#\n# PLAN-179 W2 puts a different KIND of file in the same tree. A per-plan\n# `LEDGER.md` is written INCREMENTALLY, mid-session, by the model, from\n# material that includes agent returns and tool output — and this repository\n# is PUBLIC. A blanket exemption over a machine-appended file is a standing\n# leak path, not a reviewed one, and \"the model was told to write\n# identifiers only\" is an instruction, not a control.\n#\n# The cure is a NEGATIVE exception for that ONE class rather than \"add\n# coverage\": dropping `.claude/plans/*` would drag every plan artifact into\n# the scan and regrow exactly the noise the exemption exists to remove.\n#\n# Matched by BASENAME under `.claude/plans/`, at ANY depth — deliberately\n# NOT by glob. `.claude/plans/*/LEDGER.md` misses both\n# `.claude/plans/LEDGER.md` and a ledger parked one directory deeper, and a\n# rule an author sidesteps by moving the file is not a rule.\n_NEVER_ALLOWLISTED_BASENAMES = frozenset({\n    \"LEDGER.md\",          # check_ledger_checkpoint.LEDGER_BASENAME\n    \"LEDGER-ARCHIVE.md\",  # check_ledger_checkpoint.LEDGER_ARCHIVE_BASENAME\n})\n_NEVER_ALLOWLISTED_ROOT = (\".claude\", \"plans\")\n\n\ndef is_never_allowlisted(rel_path: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"True for a file the allowlist must NOT be able to exempt.\n\n    ``rel_path`` is repo-relative and POSIX-separated. Kept a pure\n    function so the test can drive it directly and `scan()` can consult\n    it BEFORE the walker's allowlist — deny wins over allow.\n    \"\"\"\n    parts = rel_path.split(\"/\")\n    root = _NEVER_ALLOWLISTED_ROOT\n    return (\n        len(parts) > len(root)\n        and tuple(parts[: len(root)]) == root\n        and parts[-1] in _NEVER_ALLOWLISTED_BASENAMES\n    )\n\n\n# Suffixes to skip (binary / non-text files)\n_SKIP_SUFFIXES = {\n    \".png\", \".jpg\", \".jpeg\", \".gif\", \".webp\",\n    \".pdf\", \".ico\", \".zip\",\n}\n\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n# Rule 2 policy data lives with its detailed rationale; keep every mutable\n# symbol exposed on this facade for established monkeypatch-based callers.\nfrom _lib._contamination import personal_constants as _personal_constants\nfor _key, _value in vars(_personal_constants).items():\n    if _key.startswith(\"_PERSONAL_PATH_\"):\n        globals()[_key] = _value\ndel _key, _value\n\nclass PersonalPathUsage(Exception):\n    \"\"\"A CLI token this gate refuses to guess at — fatal, never ignored.\"\"\"\n\n\nclass _PersonalPathParser(argparse.ArgumentParser):\n    \"\"\"An `argparse` that refuses through THIS module's boundary.\n\n    `ArgumentParser.error()` writes its message to stderr and exits 2,\n    and that message QUOTES the offending token verbatim — measured:\n    `--emit-personal-path-rows=<value>` prints\n    `ignored explicit argument '<value>'`, and a value on this CLI can\n    BE an absolute home path (a mistyped `--root`, a stray positional).\n    A gate that refuses to quote the leak it FOUND must not quote one\n    while refusing to RUN; this was the fourth instance of that shape,\n    and it is cured where the other three were: at the boundary, by the\n    rule's own predicate.\n\n    Precision is traded for containment on purpose. `_personal_path_safe`\n    replaces the WHOLE message when it names a home directory, so the\n    operator loses the token and keeps the rc — which is the same bargain\n    every other print site in this rule makes.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    def error(self, message):        # argparse hook; always raises\n        raise PersonalPathUsage(_personal_path_safe(message))\n\n\n#: `git ls-files -s` mode of a submodule entry: it has no blob in this repository,\n#: so it is the one mode RULE 1 releases by name (and only this one).\n_TERM_GITLINK_MODE = \"160000\"\n\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n# RULE 1 (`scan`) — census of the borders where it could answer \"clean\" without\n# having looked, taken before changing it (PLAN-186-FOLLOWUP AC-7; item 11 of the\n# 1.4.4 backlog: the pack fell twice in the rail, by edges AC-7 had not listed).\n# CURED, each with a test named test_fu144_* in test_check_contamination_personal_path.py:\n#   * empty or partial walk: FileWalker turns a git failure into an empty iterator.\n#     The set of paths seen must COVER the index (a set, not a count, for the reason\n#     RULE 2 paid for in pair-rail S344 round 3);\n#   * an index that cannot be read (git missing, timeout, rc != 0): refused;\n#   * a name that is not valid UTF-8: both sides of the proof decode with\n#     errors=\"replace\", so two such names collapse into one and the sets compare equal\n#     over an unread file. Refused over the WHOLE index before any comparison; RULE 2's\n#     scope exception does not apply, because this rule's scope is every tracked path;\n#   * a tracked symlink (120000): its blob is the target STRING. read_bytes() followed\n#     it, so a term in the payload was never read and someone else's file was judged;\n#   * an index mode other than file, executable, link or gitlink: refused;\n#   * a file that exists and cannot be read, or a non-regular inode at a tracked path\n#     (a FIFO used to hang the read): rc 2. This reverses a deliberate \"fail-OPEN,\n#     INFRASTRUCTURE\" note; it is a gate, not a hook, as _personal_path_assert_enumerable\n#     already argues, so nothing here blocks a session;\n#   * a terms file that is not a regular file was read as absent (`_load_private_terms`).\n# DECLARED, not cured:\n#   * one stray non-UTF-8 byte still makes this rule skip an ordinary file whole (a term\n#     beside it goes unseen). Outside the LEDGER class that is a pinned decision, not an\n#     oversight: test_an_undecodable_non_plan_file_is_still_skipped calls it what keeps\n#     the exception narrow. Changing it is the Owner's call, not this pack's;\n#   * the path allowlist remains an exemption; all other indexed blobs are scanned;\n#   * a UTF-16 document does not match;\n#   * the index is enumerated and the worktree is read, as in RULE 2's blind spots;\n#   * a U+FFFD that is really in a tracked name is refused too (the safe direction).\n# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\n# Export extracted declarations, binding their code to the facade globals.\n# Fail-closed index proofs and monkeypatch interception retain one namespace.\nfrom types import FunctionType as _FunctionType\nfrom _lib._contamination import (\n    personal_facade_io as _personal_facade_io,\n    personal_reporting as _personal_reporting,\n    cli_checks as _cli_checks,\n)\nfor _component in (_personal_facade_io, _personal_reporting, _cli_checks):\n    for _name, _original in vars(_component).items():\n        if isinstance(_original, _FunctionType) and _original.__module__ == _component.__name__:\n            _bound = _FunctionType(\n                _original.__code__, globals(), _original.__name__,\n                _original.__defaults__, _original.__closure__,\n            )\n            _bound.__kwdefaults__ = _original.__kwdefaults__\n            _bound.__annotations__ = dict(_original.__annotations__)\n            _bound.__dict__.update(_original.__dict__)\n            _bound.__qualname__ = _original.__qualname__\n            _bound.__module__ = __name__\n            globals()[_name] = _bound\n        elif isinstance(_original, type) and _original.__module__ == _component.__name__:\n            globals()[_name] = _original\n\n_bind_personal_paths(globals(), _personal_path_scanner)\n_bind_personal_paths(globals(), _personal_path_policy)\n_personal_path_verdict_impl = personal_path_verdict\n# Publish the wrapper over the original policy implementation.\n_original = _personal_facade_io.personal_path_verdict\n_bound = _FunctionType(_original.__code__, globals(), _original.__name__,\n                       _original.__defaults__, _original.__closure__)\n_bound.__annotations__ = dict(_original.__annotations__)\n_bound.__module__ = __name__\npersonal_path_verdict = _bound\ndel _component, _name, _original, _bound\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    sys.exit(main())\n","numLines":510,"startLine":1,"totalLines":510}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_014cMFFmSjwV96YPnVgMLZQX","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
